# /etc/users/live.ns — the per-user namespace recipe for `live`, Linux line.
#
# Staged into the image as /etc/users/live.ns. hamsh main() sources
# /etc/users/<user>.ns for any shell whose uid resolves to a regular user
# (>= 1000) and falls back to /etc/users/default.ns when there is no per-user
# file. This file exists PRECISELY to stop that fallback, and the reason is
# specific to this kernel.
#
# default.ns is Hamnix's restricted recipe: it `unmount`s /var/lib/hpm,
# '#by-id' and /dev/blk out of the inherited namespace. On Hamnix that is a
# per-process namespace edit — the shell rfork'd RFNAMEG, so it is unmounting
# things out of its OWN copy and nobody else is affected. On this line the
# same recipe is wrong twice over:
#
#   1. It cannot work. `unmount` is umount2(2) here, which needs CAP_SYS_ADMIN,
#      and this shell has just dropped to uid 1001 in /etc/rc.de-user. Every
#      line would fail EPERM and print an error into the user's terminal on
#      every login.
#   2. It must not work. sys_rfork degrades to a SHARED mount namespace when
#      it cannot get CAP_SYS_ADMIN (user/linux-syscalls.c says so in as many
#      words), so a session that DID have the privilege would be unmounting
#      /dev/blk system-wide, out from under the compositor and PID 1 — a
#      regular user's login tearing down the machine's mounts.
#
# The protection default.ns is reaching for is real, but on this line it is
# delivered by file permissions and by uid checks in the servers rather than
# by namespace surgery: /var/lib/hpm and /bin are root-owned and mode 0755, so
# `live` can read them and cannot write them, and there is no /dev/blk server
# on this line at all yet. When #b lands, its uid gate is where this belongs.
#
# So: no strips. hamsh still does the useful half of the per-user path after
# sourcing us — _chdir_home() puts the session in /home/live.
echo 'ns-recipe: live (linux line) — no namespace strips; see the comment'
